      "description": "Around 65.65 lakh pilgrims visit four Char Dham shrines and Hemkund Sahib in 2026 as Badrinath portals close for winter",
      "source_name": "The Hindu National",
      "published": "2026-11-17T21:30:56"
    },
    {
      "title": "Char Dham Yatra sees 29% rise in pilgrims, 63% jump in fatalities in 2026",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/char-dham-yatra-sees-29-rise-in-pilgrims-63-jump-in-fatalities-in-2026-126111800006_1.html",
      "description": "More than 65.65 lakh pilgrims visited the Char Dham shrines and Hemkund Sahib in 2026, a 29 per cent increase over the previous year, even as pilgrim fatalities rose by 63 per cent, according to a Dehradun-based non-profit organisation.\nA total of 65,65,197 pilgrims visited the five shrines this year, compared to 51,07,346 in 2025, an increase of 14,57,851, the analysis by social and environmental non-profit SDC Foundation showed.\nAmong the shrines, Badrinath recorded the highest footfall at 23,49,345, followed by Kedarnath (19,33,138), Gangotri (10,07,177), Yamunotri (8,84,841) and Hemkund Sahib (3,90,696).\nThe NGO's analysis of the state government's daily bulletins found that pilgrim numbers increased across all five shrines, but at varying rates. Praveen Upreti of SDC Foundation, along with its founder Anoop Nautiyal, collated the data.\nBadrinath and Hemkund Sahib reported the highest growth at 42 per cent each, followed by Yamunotri (37 per cent) and Gangotri (33 per cent). ..",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-18T02:33:36"
    }
  ]
